# --no-stash is load-bearing, not a convenience.
#
# By default lint-staged stashes the entire working tree before running, then
# restores it afterwards. With a dev server up, that rewrite races the file
# watcher: `tsx --watch` sees src/server/main.ts change mid-stash, restarts into
# a module graph that is momentarily inconsistent, dies before printing a single
# line, and then PARKS — "Failed running 'src/server/main.ts'. Waiting for file
# changes before restarting..." It never exits, so no supervisor notices, and
# the dashboard is simply down until someone restarts it by hand.
#
# The stash is also a real risk here on its own terms: this tree routinely
# carries 100+ modified and untracked files, and a stash/restore cycle of that
# size on every commit is a lot of moving parts sitting between in-progress work
# and the disk.
#
# What is given up: on a lint failure the working tree keeps whatever
# `eslint --fix` already wrote, instead of being rolled back. That is visible in
# `git diff` and recoverable; a silently dead dev server was neither.
npx lint-staged --no-stash
